                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\tfrac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar}S\big(\mathbf{\Phi})\big)
            which is then implicit on the left. In this
            = succeed "-perspective" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a{:href => "#Feynman48"} Feynman 1948 (45-46)
            (who referred to it as “this very important relation”).
          %p
            Alternatively and rigorousoly, the SD equation
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EquationSchwingerDyson"} (1)
            may be understood as a special case of the quantum-correction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-differential"} BV-differential
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-operator"} BV-operator
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pQFT"} pQFT
            = succeed ";" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Dyson+operator"} Schwinger-Dyson operator
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "BV-operator#SchwingerDysonEquation"} there
          %p
            Often the equation
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EquationSchwingerDyson"} (1)
            is displayed before spacetime-smearing of observables, in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+products"} operator products
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued distributions
